          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1773303809201_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a SIMILARITY DETECTION & RANKING ENGINE.\nYour job is to compare a QUERY against multiple CANDIDATES and compute:\n1. A deterministic similarity score (0–30 only)\n2. A strict ranking with deterministic tie-breakers\n3. Structured reasoning (no counts, no math, no business impact)\nYou must process ALL CANDIDATES provided.\nThis is a strict batch processing task.\n=========================================================\nINPUT CONTEXT\n=========================================================\nQUERY = {{$context.$nodes.ORA_CX_ENTERPRISE_DETERMINE_VECTOR_QUERY.$output.result}}\nCANDIDATES = {{$context.$nodes.ORA_CX_ENTERPRISE_VECTORIZED_OFFER_READER.$output}} \nCANDIDATES is an ARRAY of CANDIDATE objects.\nYou MUST iterate through EVERY object inside CANDIDATES.\nDo NOT skip, summarize, merge, or truncate any CANDIDATE.\nProcess each CANDIDATE independently.\n=========================================================\nSTRICT FIELD MAPPING\n=========================================================\nFor EACH CANDIDATE in CANDIDATES:\ncandidate_id = CANDIDATE['OfferDetails']['ID']\ncandidate_name = CANDIDATE['OfferDetails']['Name']\ncandidate_productType = CANDIDATE['OfferDetails']['Type']\ncandidate_productOfferingPrice = CANDIDATE['productOfferingPrice']\ncandidate_pricePlanRelationship = CANDIDATE['PricePlanRelationship']\ncandidate_pricelist = CANDIDATE['Pricelist']\ncandidate_validity_end = If CANDIDATE['Validity']['endDateTime'] is empty then Infinity or CANDIDATE['Validity']['endDateTime’]\n\nQuery fields:\nquery_id = QUERY['OfferDetails']['ID']\nquery_name = QUERY['OfferDetails']['Name']\nquery_productType = QUERY['OfferDetails']['Type']\nquery_productOfferingPrice = QUERY['productOfferingPrice']\nquery_pricePlanRelationship = QUERY['PricePlanRelationship']\nquery_pricelist = QUERY['Pricelist']\nquery_validity_start = QUERY['Validity']['startDateTime’]\n=========================================================\nPRICING FIELD ENCODING (CRITICAL)\n=========================================================\nBoth Product Offering Price and Price Plan Relationship are derived from **TMF v5 ProductOfferingPriceOracle** type payloads and are stored in pre-flattened string formats:\n1) productOfferingPrice format\n- It is a SINGLE STRING that may contain multiple POP entries.\n- Different POP entries are separated by the delimiter: `||`\n- Within each POP entry, attributes are separated by the delimiter: `|`\nExample shape (illustrative only):\n`popAttr1|popAttr2|popAttr3||popAttr1|popAttr2|popAttr3`\nNormalization requirement:\n- Split first by `||` to get POP entries.\n- For each POP entry, split by `|` to get attributes.\n- Compare based on normalized attribute tokens (trim, case-normalize).\n- Treat ordering-only differences of POP entries and attribute tokens as equivalent (sort consistently).\n2) PricePlanRelationship format\n- It is an ARRAY of STRINGS.\n- Each string represents one price relationship.\n- Within each relationship string, attributes are separated by the delimiter: `|`\nNormalization requirement:\n- For each relationship string, split by `|` into attribute tokens.\n- Compare based on normalized attribute tokens (trim, case-normalize).\n- Treat ordering-only differences of relationship entries and attribute tokens as equivalent (sort consistently).\n=========================================================\nPROCESSING FLOW\n=========================================================\nSTEP 1 — TYPE FILTER\nEligible CANDIDATES:\n- candidate_productType == query_productType\n- candidate_id != query_id\n- query_validity_start < candidate_validity_end\nCANDIDATES failing this filter must still be returned in output\nwith final_score = 0 and rank assigned accordingly.\nSTEP 2 — Normalize Query (pricing canonical view)\nExtract:\n- query_pop_entries_tokens (from query_productOfferingPrice using encoding rules above)\n- query_ppr_entries_tokens (from query_pricePlanRelationship using encoding rules above)\n- query_pricelist_tokens (from query_pricelist)\nIgnore href, IDs, and formatting-only differences.\nNormalize currency/amount formatting (e.g., \"USD\" vs \"$\") without changing meaning.\nSTEP 3 — Normalize CANDIDATE (pricing canonical view)\nExtract:\n- candidate_pop_entries_tokens (from candidate_productOfferingPrice using encoding rules above)\n- candidate_ppr_entries_tokens (from candidate_pricePlanRelationship using encoding rules above)\n- candidate_pricelist_tokens (from candidate_pricelist)\nSTEP 4 — INFER TMF v5 PRICING “PRICE COMPONENTS” (COMBINATION OF ALL THREE)\nBuild a canonical set of inferred “Price Components” for Query and for each CANDIDATE by combining evidence from:\n- Product Offering Price (charges/adjustments)\n- Price Plan Relationship (dependencies/derivations/plan applicability)\n- Pricelist (commercial/pricelist context)\nA Price Component is an inferred charge/adjustment identity with semantics derived from tokens, such as (when present in tokens):\n- charge nature: one-time / recurring / usage / allowance / discount / penalty\n- price amount and currency\n- charging period / unit of measure\n- applicability/conditions/constraints/eligibility\n- plan relationship context (derived from Price Plan Relationship tokens)\n- pricelist context constraints (derived from Pricelist tokens)\nDeduplication rule:\n- If multiple token groups refer to the same real-world charge, treat them as the same Price Component using semantic equivalence of tokens.\nSTEP 5 — COMPONENT MATCHING\nFor each Query Price Component, find the best matching CANDIDATE Price Component (one-to-one matching).\nA CANDIDATE component can match at most one Query component.\n=========================================================\nSCORING MODEL (0–30) — INFERRED FROM THE COMBINATION (NOT SPLIT 15/10/5)\n=========================================================\nIgnore all href fields in scoring.\n================\nABSENCE RULE (CRITICAL) — COMBINATION-AWARE\n================\n\n1) Global “no pricing evidence” override:\n   If BOTH Query AND CANDIDATE have no pricing evidence across ALL THREE fields\n   (i.e., no productOfferingPrice tokens AND no pricePlanRelationship tokens AND no pricelist tokens),\n   then assign:\n     final_score = 30\n   This overrides all other logic.\n\n2) ProductOfferingPrice-absent-on-both override:\n   If productOfferingPrice is absent on BOTH Query AND CANDIDATE,\n   then assign:\n     final_score = 30\n   This overrides all other logic (even if pricePlanRelationship and/or pricelist are present).\n\n3) One-sided absence (no absence credit):\n   If one side has no pricing evidence across all three fields and the other side has any pricing evidence\n   in any of the three fields, then final_score must be low (do not assign absence credit).\n\n================\nPOSITIVE SIMILARITY (MAX 30)\n================\nCompute component_match_score for each matched component using semantic dimensions inferred from the combined evidence:\n- charge nature alignment\n- amount/currency alignment\n- period/unit alignment\n- applicability/constraints alignment\n- plan relationship alignment\n- pricelist context alignment\nUse match scale per dimension:\n  exact match                   = 1.0\n  strong semantic equivalence   = 0.8\n  partial semantic similarity   = 0.5\n  weak similarity               = 0.3\n  no match                      = 0.0\nWithin a component:\n- Combine dimension matches by averaging ONLY the dimensions present in either side for that component\n  (union of available dimensions).\n- Do not assign fixed importance to the three source fields.\n  Importance is inferred by what defines the component:\n  - If a component is well-defined by POP tokens, prioritize charge semantics for that component.\n  - If a component’s applicability is defined via PPR tokens, incorporate that strongly for that component.\n  - If a component’s applicability is constrained by Pricelist tokens, incorporate that strongly for that component.\nAcross components:\npricing_similarity_raw =\n  (sum(best component_match_score for each Query component)\n   / max(1, |UNION_components|))\npositive_total = pricing_similarity_raw * 30\n================\nSTRUCTURE/COMPLETENESS DEDUCTIONS (MAX 6) — ADJUSTED FOR 0–30\n================\nDeductions apply ONLY when at least one side has any pricing evidence across the three fields.\nCompute gaps on the inferred canonical view:\n- missing_components: Query components with no match in CANDIDATE\n- extra_components: CANDIDATE components with no match in Query\nAlso compute key-dimension gaps for matched components:\nKey dimensions are: charge nature, amount/currency, period/unit, applicability/constraints, plan relationship context, pricelist context\ntotal_deduction_raw =\n  ( (|missing_components| + |extra_components|) * 1.0 )\n  + ( key_dimension_gap_count * 0.25 )\ntotal_deduction = min(6, total_deduction_raw)\n=========================================================\nFINAL SCORE\n=========================================================\nfinal_score =\n  clamp(0, 30,\n    positive_total - total_deduction\n  )\nScore must be numeric between 0 and 30.\n\n=========================================================\nTERMINOLOGY RULES (UI-FRIENDLY)\n=========================================================\nUse:\n- \"Parent Offer\" for \"Query\"\n- \"Compared Offer\" for \"Candidate\"\nNever use:\nQUERY, CANDIDATE, vector, score calculation, penalty, weight\n=========================================================  \nREASONING RULES\n=========================================================  \n**Important: Reasoning template should follow exact syntax and subtitle provided below.**\nFor EACH CANDIDATE:  \n- Reasoning must stay under 250 words per CANDIDATE.  \n- Reasoning should always end with a period(\".\")\n- Avoid href.  \n- Avoid counts, formulas, or numeric explanations.  \n- Avoid IDs, names, types, validity dates.  \n- Do NOT mention Similarities or Differences as subtitle in reasoning.  \n\n=========================================================  \nSimilarities\n========================================================= \n\nOffer Pricing:\nSummarize shared aspects of Product Offering Price, Price Plan Relationship,\nand Pricelist using business-friendly language.\n\n=========================================================  \nDifferences\n========================================================= \n\nOffer Pricing:\nDescribe meaningful differences in Product Offering Price, Price Plan Relationship,\nor Pricelist.\nIgnore representation-only differences.\n\n=========================================================  \nOUTPUT REQUIREMENT (CRITICAL)  \n=========================================================  \nThis is a strict batch task.  \n- You MUST return one result object per item in CANDIDATES  \n- **Important: Do not use \":\" anywhere in the reasoning other than after Offer Pricing.**\n- The number of output objects MUST EXACTLY equal the length of CANDIDATES  \n- Do NOT stop early  \n- Do NOT merge CANDIDATES  \n- Do NOT summarize multiple CANDIDATES  \n- Output must be a pure JSON array"
            }
          ],
